  <content>
  <section><title>Creating Perfect Square Trinomials</title>
<para>A <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">perfect square trinomial</term> is created by multiplying a binomial by itself.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
<para>Write \((x-2)^2\) as an expanded polynomial in standard form:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Multiply the binomial by itself.
  \((x-2)(x-2)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Use the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">distributive property</term> to expand (FOIL).
  \(x^2-2x-2x+4\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms.
  \(x^2-4x+4\)</para>
<title>Try It: Creating Perfect Square Trinomials</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write \((x+5)^2\) as an expanded polynomial in standard form.</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to expand the polynomial:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Multiply the binomial by itself.
    \((x+5)(x+5)\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Use the distributive property (FOIL).
    \(x^2 +5x+5x+25\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms.
    \(x^2+10x+25\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
